Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Low x-limit and singularity", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.964027003414208*^9, 3.964027015454266*^9}, {
  3.964027063374372*^9, 
  3.964027063439386*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a6aa586d-7a78-4415-ab0a-\
b867f015da64"],

Cell["\<\
However, as x approaches 0, the redshift approaches 1/0. \
I\[CloseCurlyQuote]ve tried to find a finite limit, but I don\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]t think there is one.
I\[CloseCurlyQuote]m pretty sure the assumptions we make \
don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t hold for very small values of x. I think this is \
because the corresponding photons pierced the GW\[CloseCurlyQuote]s spherical \
wavefront very close to the GW source and very soon after the merger event.
I\[CloseCurlyQuote]ve done some experimenting, and the redshift values become \
unreasonably large around x = 1E-13. For a GW source at a typical distance of \
a billion light years away, this correlates to roughly an hour after it \
passes. (The equation doesn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t hold for times right after the \
GW is received at Earth). I\[CloseCurlyQuote]m not sure how to justify where \
to put the cutoff, other than the equation becomes unreasonably large.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.964027046631825*^9},ExpressionUUID->"904ed703-797b-4faf-9d01-\
0d3c0c27b208"],

